y. A benzene-degrading Pseudomonas became highly enriched and consumed all available oxygen. The number of ORM2 cells plummeted during this time and re-growth and associated recovery of methanogenic benzene degradation took almost one year. These results have quite profound implications when considering impact of transient oxygen exposure in field environments. Moreover, the methanogenic benzene degrading pathway is fundamentally different from reported aerobic pathways, carried out by distinct microbial communities, and without oxygen involvement.</STUDY_ABSTRACT></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="Bottle2_Day98" accession="SRS12021236"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS12021236</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN25976516</EXTERNAL_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="pda|ctot" label="Sample name">Bottle2_Day98</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>Bottle2_Day98</TITLE><SAMPLE_NAME><TAXON_ID>1704045</TAXON_ID><SCIENTIFIC_NAME>aquifer metagenome</SCIENTIFIC_NAME></SAMPLE_NAME><DESCRIPTION>Bottle 2; active microcosm containing the methanogenic benzene-degrading enrichment culture DGG-B, 98 days of incuabtion, amended with 0.1 mL air on Day 53, amended with 10 mL air on Days 94 and 224, bottle presentally aerobic, benzene degradation has stalled</DESCRIPTION><SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_LINK><XREF_LINK><DB>bioproject</DB><ID>807302</ID><LABEL>PRJNA807302</LABEL></XREF_LINK></SAMPLE_LINK></SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>collection_date</TAG><VALUE>2018-06-25</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>depth</TAG><VALUE>0.3 - 0.7 m</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>elev</TAG><VALUE>300 m</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>env_broad_scale</TAG><VALUE>Groundwater aquifer [ENVO:00012408]</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>env_local_scale</TAG><VALUE>Petroleum-enriched sediments [ENVO:00002115]</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>env_medium</TAG><VALUE>Sediment [ENVO:00002007]</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>geo_loc_name</TAG><VALUE>USA: Oklahoma, oil refinery</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>lat_lon</TAG><VALUE>36 N 97 W</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>isolation_source</TAG><VALUE>Contaminated sediments from an oil refinery in Oklahoma, USA (Nales et al., 1998; Bioremediat J 2). The site was described as being from a saturated, highly anaerobic zone that had been chronically exposed to hydrocarbons.</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>oxy_stat_samp</TAG><VALUE>aerobic</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>samp_collect_device</TAG><VALUE>The cell pellet (10,000 x g centrifugation, 15 min) from 1 mL culture was frozen at -80 C for later DNA extraction using the Dneasy PowerSoil Kit (Qiagen) according to the manufacturer's procedure.</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>samp_store_dur</TAG><VALUE>-80</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>temp</TAG><VALUE>23 C</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>Benzene degradation activity</TAG><VALUE>Stalled</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>Total bacteria (16S rRNA gene copies/mL)</TAG><VALUE>7.67E+07</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>Total archaea (16S rRNA gene copies/mL)</TAG><VALUE>1.85E+07</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>ORM2 (16S rRNA gene copies/mL)</TAG><VALUE>4.98E+07</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>OD1 (16S rRNA gene copies/mL)</TAG><VALUE>5.34E+06</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>BioSampleModel</TAG><VALUE>MIMARKS.survey</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>BioSampleModel</TAG><VALUE>MIGS/MIMS/MIMARKS.sediment</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES></SAMPLE><Pool><Member member_name="" accession="SRS12021236" sample_name="Bottle2_Day98" sample_title="Bottle2_Day98" spots="143689" bases="86213400" tax_id="1704045" organism="aquifer metagenome"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS12021236</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespa