lly derived decades ago from oil-contaminated sediment. A 1,200-day study was performed to test how small amounts of oxygen would affect degradation rates and impact its microbial community composition. The culture includes a benzene fermenter from Deltaproteobacteria Candidate clade Sva0485 (referred to as ORM2) and methanogenic archaea. Adding oxygen caused drastic perturbation of the microbial community. A benzene-degrading Pseudomonas became highly enriched and consumed all available oxygen. The number of ORM2 cells plummeted during this time and re-growth and associated recovery of methanogenic benzene degradation took almost one year. These results have quite profound implications when considering impact of transient oxygen exposure in field environments. Moreover, the methanogenic benzene degrading pathway is fundamentally different from reported aerobic pathways, carried out by distinct microbial communities, and without oxygen involvement.</STUDY_ABSTRACT></DESCRIPTOR></STUDY><SAMPLE alias="Bottle1_Day259" accession="SRS12021231"><IDENTIFIERS><PRIMARY_ID>SRS12021231</PRIMARY_ID><EXTERNAL_ID namespace="BioSample">SAMN25976510</EXTERNAL_ID><SUBMITTER_ID namespace="pda|ctot" label="Sample name">Bottle1_Day259</SUBMITTER_ID></IDENTIFIERS><TITLE>Bottle1_Day259</TITLE><SAMPLE_NAME><TAXON_ID>1704045</TAXON_ID><SCIENTIFIC_NAME>aquifer metagenome</SCIENTIFIC_NAME></SAMPLE_NAME><DESCRIPTION>Bottle 1; active microcosm containing the methanogenic benzene-degrading enrichment culture DGG-B, 259 days of incuabtion, amended with 0.1 mL air on Day 39, amended with 10 mL air on Day 94, bottle presentally anaerobic, benzene degradation remains stalled</DESCRIPTION><SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_LINK><XREF_LINK><DB>bioproject</DB><ID>807302</ID><LABEL>PRJNA807302</LABEL></XREF_LINK></SAMPLE_LINK></SAMPLE_LINKS><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTES><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>collection_date</TAG><VALUE>2018-12-03</VALUE></SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><SAMPLE_ATTRIBUTE><TAG>depth</TAG><VALUE>0.3 - 0.7 m</VALUE></SAMPLE_A